  $title = "$DIR Software Files";
  include $_SERVER{'DOCUMENT_ROOT'}.'/pdp8/header.php';
?>
<BODY><FONT size=4>
<P>
This is an archive of the DEC software titles.
<P>
Since the files are organized by DEC part number, a 
word about part numbers is probably in order.  A DEC part number
might look like any of these:
<pre>
        dec-812-pb
        dec-08-d01a-pb
        dec-08-d01a-b-pb
        ac-6527d-ma
</pre>
depending on the vintage of the title in question.
<P>
What's been done here, is to organize the DEC software part numbers into
directories based on the program being addressed.  For example,
directory "dec-08-cdd" contains files for all part numbers associated with
any version of DDT-8.  
<P>
The directory names generally are the names of the "old" dec part number,
where available, with the version letter removed.  These start with "dec-", 
or "maindec-".
<P>A number of newer titles don't have a name like that.  For those, a
name starting with "xx-" has been used.  If the file is from the diagnostics
group, the directory name will be similar to "xx-08-dhdka".  Otherwise, the
directory name is just "xx-yyyy", where yyyy is the part number DEC used, with
the media type stripped off the front, and the version number, license type, 
and CPU type all stripped off the back.  (So, the "AA-0690C-TA" COS-310
manual gets put in "xx-0690", etc.)
<P>
(The prefix "xx-" was chosen (over "ax-") because not all the media types
start with "A" anymore in the July 1979 edition of the Software Components
Catalog.)
<P>
A link indicates the file is currently available here.  Otherwise, the file
is still named, but not clickable.  (If it's not named here, it's either not
listed in the Software Components Catalog, or it has a media type I haven't
decided how to deal with yet.)
<P>
This is a big list, so you'll probably want to use "search in page".
<P>
